unicode-tag(1) -- tag deceptive Unicode in text for safe machine reading
========================================================================

<!--
# Copyright (C) 2026 - 2026 ENCRYPTED SUPPORT LLC <adrelanos@whonix.org>
# See the file COPYING for copying conditions.
-->

## SYNOPSIS

`unicode-tag` [<FILE>...]

## DESCRIPTION

unicode-tag reads text -- from <FILE> arguments, or from standard input when
none are given -- and writes it back byte-for-byte, except that every Unicode
code point which could DECEIVE a reader is replaced, in place, by a
self-describing `[U+XXXX NAME]` token: bidi controls that reorder text,
zero-width and invisible characters, C0/C1 and DEL controls (including carriage
return and ESC), and cross-script homoglyphs posing as ASCII (scoped to a
mixed-script token, so an honest foreign word is left untouched).

Honest text -- ASCII, accented Latin, CJK, emoji, whole-script foreign words --
passes unchanged. Newline and tab pass verbatim and a trailing newline is
neither added nor removed. An undecodable byte is tagged, not dropped.

It is meant for feeding untrusted text to a program or model that must not be
fooled by, or silently relay, an active control code point.

## EXIT STATUS

0 on success; 1 on an unreadable file or a closed output pipe.

## SEE ALSO

`unicode-tag-stdin`(1), `secure-terminal-cli`(1)
